    "cleaned_summary": "Based on reports and data published strictly before September 8, 2026, the following details outline the OPEC+ crude oil supply decisions, production responses, and inventory levels:\n\n### OPEC+ Production Decisions (September 2026)\n* **October Quota Freeze:** On September 6, 2026, seven core OPEC+ countries\u2014Saudi Arabia, Russia, Iraq, Kuwait, Kazakhstan, Algeria, and Oman\u2014held a virtual meeting and decided to maintain their September required production levels through October 2026, keeping output quotas unchanged. \n* **September Increase Completed:** This decision paused a series of planned output increases. In September 2026, OPEC+ had implemented a scheduled 188,000 barrels per day (bpd) production increase, which completed the phased rollback of a 1.65 million bpd voluntary supply cut first introduced in 2023.\n* **Remaining Cuts:** A separate layer of production cuts totaling approximately 2 million bpd remains in place for the broader OPEC+ group through the end of 2026.\n* **Focus on 2027:** The pause in production increases allows the alliance to review members' production capacities and negotiate production baselines and quotas for 2027. The next monthly meeting is scheduled for October 4, 2026.\n\n### Supply Response and Geopolitical Context\n* **Strait of Hormuz Disruptions:** The decision to pause production increases comes amid severe geopolitical disruptions, particularly the ongoing conflict involving Iran and the Strait of Hormuz, which has restricted tanker flows and physical crude exports.\n* **Actual Production vs. Targets:** Due to these disruptions, actual physical oil production by OPEC+ members has remained significantly below their official targets. The International Energy Agency (IEA) reported in August 2026 that approximately 8.3 million bpd of Gulf output remained shut in.\n\n### Global and OECD Oil Inventories\n* **Global Inventory Plunge:** According to the IEA's August 2026 Oil Market Report, global observed oil inventories plunged by 69 million barrels in July 2026. This decline was primarily driven by lower volumes of oil on water due to export disruptions in the Gulf and Caspian Sea, rather than onshore stock draws (which fell by only 6 million barrels).\n* **OECD Commercial Stocks:** OPEC's August 2026 Monthly Oil Market Report (MOMR) indicated that preliminary June 2026 data showed OECD commercial oil inventories dropped by 26.4 million barrels month-on-month to 2,729 million barrels. This level was 59.6 million barrels lower year-on-year and 66.5 million barrels below the latest five-year average.\n* **EIA Projections:** The U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) estimated in its August 2026 Short-Term Energy Outlook (STEO) that global oil inventories fell by an average of 4.2 million bpd in Q2 2026 and projected an additional decline of 3.8 million bpd on average in Q3 2026.\n\n***\n\n**Sources:**\n* OPEC September 6, 2026 Press Release: https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEk_vbYAxJBLnixTJ7KZES49Q2yWafQoxHNWoYS1b-ECv8xi76TJ7F2Fz9pr8viXY2UBNL5ZUa9zKgo96EMaJyHORdfB7M6vIuFZS72bOVhtdE7mqBJVBRAMkuy1NbuaWA6HTUATE_diRaNvde9dhiGKA==\n* The Astana Times (September 7, 2026): https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHkJmrosjjRBmM51WPtEPEUaBErGMX3OFC4kdNkUT6Gbjvs4o_udk_Z-6ZrYqchMqqsqDo5Xq0kdGmFCKm3jTODhGG12IXTUzSG_y4cpUQ3Uv1vq1o1_KWKgVJGH_9sukuRYNcx9mhGVFWBfqMBjae4ulWy3TxGaiyVojTqeW5RDZYaazUoavkqGCgvUgJYbxykaWbEFBubm5d0H9w=\n* Egypt Oil & Gas (September 6, 2026): https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEp580FNOhF4csNY8f4MTHSO_ZvRUpinboVxoEY_yun_fmSL9gmxeOjiXp25dn7sKfplg",
